# flipped 2026-08-06: preflight reported GO against production — every
# RLS-enabled tenant table has a policy, and databridge_app holds every grant.
# Row-level security is now actually enforced rather than bypassed.

# Which PostgreSQL role the backend connects as.
#
#   admin  →  databridge       (superuser; BYPASSES row-level security)
#   app    →  databridge_app   (NOBYPASSRLS; RLS is actually enforced)
#
# `admin` is what production ran from the beginning, and it meant row-level
# security enforced NOTHING: a superuser bypasses RLS unconditionally, and
# FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY binds the table owner, not a superuser. Isolation
# rested entirely on the application's own tenant filters and the
# denyUnlessOwned gates. Setting this to `app` is what makes the policies real.
#
# BEFORE setting this to `app`, run the preflight against production:
#
#   cd backend
#   DATABASE_URL='<admin url>' npx tsx scripts/preflight-role-flip.ts
#
# It exits 0 only when every tenant-scoped table has a policy and the role holds
# every grant it needs. The flip workflow runs it again itself and refuses to
# proceed on a NO-GO, so this is a courtesy check, not the only one.
#
# The flip requires ONE GitHub secret: DB_APP_PASSWORD, a password you choose.
# The workflow sets it on the role, proves the role can read real tables, and
# derives the connection string from the admin one. It fails with a clear
# message if the secret is missing rather than deploying a broken revision.
#
# Rollback is this same one-line edit back to `admin`, and the workflow also
# rolls back on its own if the new revision cannot read the database.
